rect algorithm, but this is the code under test \u2014 it's the buggy version from HumanEval? Actually this is the correct HumanEval implementation of minSubArraySum:\n\n```python\ndef minSubArraySum(nums):\n    max_sum = 0\n    s = 0\n    for num in nums:\n        s += -num\n        if (s < 0):\n            s = 0\n        max_sum = max(s, max_sum)\n    if max_sum == 0:\n        max_sum = max(-i for i in nums)\n    min_sum = -max_sum\n    return min_sum\n```\n\nWait, this algorithm computes max subarray sum of negated array... Hmm, actually the negation trick: min subarray sum = -max subarray sum of negated array. But the reset `if s < 0: s = 0` finds maximum subarray sum (Kadane with reset when negative). Negating: min subarray sum of nums = -(max subarray sum of -nums). Kadane on -nums: s accumulates -num, reset to 0 if s < 0 (Kadane variant), max_sum = max subarray sum. So yes, this computes -(max subarray sum of negated nums) = min subarray sum. Correct.\n\nFor [-3, -2, -5]: max subarray sum of [3,2,5] = 10. Trace: s=3, max=3; s=5, max=5; s=10, max=10. Wait I made an error: s starts at 0, num=-3: s = 0 + 3 = 3, not <0. num=-2: s = 5. num=-5: s = 10. max_sum=10. min_sum = -10. Correct!\n\nI made a sign error earlier. Let me retrace case 1: nums=[2,3,4,1,2,4], negated = [-2,-3,-4,-1,-2,-4], max subarray sum of all negatives = max single element = -1. So min_sum = 1. Correct.\n\nSo for coverage:\n- Case with mixed signs where s goes negative sometimes and stays non-negative other times: covers s<0 both branches and max_sum != 0 branch.\n- Case with all positive numbers: covers max_sum == 0 branch (line 11).\n- Empty list? nums=[] \u2192 loop doesn't run, max_sum=0, then max() over empty generator \u2192 ValueError. Avoid that, or maybe test it. Not needed for coverage; skip.\n\nLet me design tests:\n\nTest 1: `minSubArraySum([2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 4]) == 1` \u2014 covers line 11 (max_sum == 0 branch), s < 0 true branch.\n\nTest 2: `minSubArraySum([-1, -2, -3]) == -6` \u20